Assets are resources; liabilities are obligations; equity is the residual claim.|Assets = liabilities + equity.|₹200 assets funded by ₹100 liabilities leaves ₹100 equity.|Balanced books can still contain valuation errors.|Collect an existing ₹5 receivable. Total assets…?|Stay unchanged|Rise ₹5|Fall ₹5
Double-entry records each transaction with equal total debits and credits.|Entries preserve the accounting equation across linked accounts.|Buy ₹10 inventory on credit: debit inventory, credit trade payables.|Equal entries do not prove correct recognition or measurement.|This credit purchase immediately affects cash by…?|Zero|−₹10|+₹10
Debits and credits are account-entry directions, not synonyms for good and bad.|Asset increases usually debit; liability and equity increases usually credit.|Receive a ₹10 loan: debit cash and credit borrowings.|Expense accounts and revenue accounts follow different normal balances.|Repaying principal normally requires…?|Debit debt and credit cash|Debit revenue and credit cash|Debit cash and credit expense
Journals record transactions; ledgers group accounts; trial balances compare debit and credit totals; closing entries reset temporary accounts.|Trace document → journal → ledger → trial balance → statements.|A sale moves from invoice to revenue ledger and ultimately period profit.|A balanced trial balance can omit an entire transaction.|Can equal debit and credit totals prove no omissions?|No|Yes|Only at year-end
Accrual accounting recognises economic events under applicable rules; cash accounting follows receipts and payments.|A recognised credit sale affects profit before cash collection.|Deliver ₹10 goods on credit today; collection next month swaps receivables for cash.|Accrual does not permit recognising unsupported expected sales.|Does collection create the same revenue again?|No|Yes|Only if paid late
Revenue follows performance; expenses follow applicable recognition, consumption, and obligation rules.|Under IFRS 15, recognise revenue as performance obligations are satisfied.|Prepaid ₹10 for undelivered goods can create a contract liability.|Cash receipt alone does not prove revenue.|A customer prepayment necessarily increases current profit?|No|Yes|Only when large
Accruals record incurred amounts before payment; deferrals postpone recognition; prepayments are paid-before-use assets; provisions cover qualifying uncertain obligations.|Timing differences create balance-sheet accounts until settlement or consumption.|Pay ₹12 annual rent upfront: expense ₹1 monthly and reduce the prepayment.|A provision is not a discretionary profit-smoothing reserve.|After one month, the rent prepayment is…?|₹11|₹12|Zero
Capex acquires or improves qualifying long-lived assets; opex is recognised as expenses under relevant rules.|Capitalisation changes expense timing, not whether cash was spent.|A ₹20 machine may be depreciated; routine maintenance is ordinarily expensed.|Calling spending strategic does not make it capitalisable.|Which normally needs immediate expense treatment?|Routine maintenance|A qualifying machine purchase|All asset construction costs
Capitalisation records a qualifying asset; immediate expensing charges the current period.|Only eligible costs meeting recognition criteria become assets.|Capitalising ₹10 rather than expensing it raises current pre-tax profit by ₹10 before depreciation.|Future depreciation or impairment can reverse the apparent benefit.|Does capitalisation itself create additional cash?|No|Yes|Only for profitable firms
Historical cost uses transaction-based measures; fair value reflects an orderly market exit price; amortised cost follows effective-interest allocation.|Classification and the relevant standard determine the measurement basis.|A ₹95 debt asset with contractual ₹100 repayment accretes under an effective-interest model.|These measures are not interchangeable analyst preferences.|Which basis is tied to a current market exit price?|Fair value|Original invoice cost|Accumulated depreciation
Materiality considers decision relevance; prudence supports cautious judgment; consistency aids comparison; going concern assumes continuing operations.|Apply principles alongside specific recognition and measurement requirements.|A small error can be material if it changes compliance with a covenant.|Prudence does not justify deliberate understatement.|Can a small amount still be material?|Yes, because context matters|No, only absolute size matters|Only when cash is involved
Estimates use uncertain inputs; policies choose accounting bases; errors misapply available information.|IFRS generally treats estimate changes prospectively; policy changes and material errors often retrospectively, subject to rules.|Revising useful life changes future depreciation; an arithmetic mistake is an error.|Do not relabel an error as a new estimate.|A revised useful life based on new information is normally…?|An estimate change|An owner contribution|A cash-flow correction only
Reporting periods define the interval; comparative statements show prior-period information.|Match comparable lengths, policies, and seasonal timing.|Compare a six-month profit with the prior six months rather than an unmatched full year.|A fiscal-year change can distort apparent growth.|A nine-month period versus twelve months is…?|Not directly comparable without adjustment|Always a growth decline|Always comparable if audited
Standalone accounts show one legal entity; consolidated accounts portray the controlled group as an economic entity.|Consolidation eliminates internal balances and transactions.|A parent sells ₹10 to its subsidiary: group revenue does not rise merely from that internal sale.|Separate-entity profit and group profit differ.|Internal group sales should normally be…?|Eliminated on consolidation|Counted twice|Added to external growth
Ind AS, IFRS, and US GAAP are distinct reporting systems with overlapping principles and specific differences.|Identify the framework, effective period, and relevant standard before comparing.|Lease presentation and development-cost treatment can differ across frameworks.|Similar terminology does not ensure identical accounting.|For a cross-framework peer comparison, first inspect…?|Policies and measurement differences|Only share price|Only company age
Annual reports combine audited statements, notes, governance, and narrative; interim and regulatory filings provide additional updates.|Read notes and auditor reports alongside management's discussion.|A headline debt figure may omit lease details disclosed in a note.|A presentation slide is not a substitute for complete filings.|Where should you seek an accounting-policy explanation?|The financial-statement notes|Only the logo|Only a social-media post
